<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Differential-testing finding (issue #317), FIXED in issue #325: an
  RDF/XML property element whose local name is non-ASCII (here, Japanese)
  caused Factoidal's RDF/XML parser to silently return ZERO triples and
  exit code 0, printing no error. pyoxigraph and rdflib both parse this
  file and produce the expected single triple. Repro:

    bin/linux-x86_64/factoidal count tools/difftest/repros/rdfxml_unicode_qname_dropped.rdf
    # before #325: "... : 0 triples", which is wrong; should be 1
    # python3 -c "import pyoxigraph as ox; print(list(ox.parse(path='...', format=ox.RdfFormat.RDF_XML)))"
    #   yields 1 quad, as expected

  Root cause: Parser.XML.is_name_char is a BYTE test over a UTF-8 stream
  and accepted only lead bytes (0xC0 and above), rejecting continuation
  bytes (0x80 to 0xBF). The Name scan therefore stopped INSIDE the first
  non-ASCII codepoint, the element failed to parse, and the failure
  propagated to the document root.

  This comment was rewritten on 2026-07-30. The original contained
  double-hyphen sequences, which XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids anywhere
  inside a comment, so the fixture was not well-formed XML and Factoidal
  correctly rejected it for that SECOND, independent reason. pyoxigraph
  and rdflib are lenient about it and so did not surface the fixture
  defect. Keep this file free of double hyphens.

  See docs/designissues/2026-07-29-differential-testing-ledger.md,
  finding 1, and its resolution section.
-->
<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
         xmlns:ex="http://example.org/onto#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/s">
    <ex:日本語 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">1.0E-300</ex:日本語>
  </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>
